1
00:04:09,959 --> 00:04:11,377
Claire.

2
00:04:26,392 --> 00:04:28,144
I miss you.

3
00:05:35,461 --> 00:05:36,796
Starck: Dr. Weir,

4
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:40,133
report to the Lewis & Clark
in docking bay four.

5
00:05:57,108 --> 00:05:58,693
Justin: Ion drive set.

6
00:05:58,776 --> 00:06:00,153
Smith: Thank you.

7
00:06:01,613 --> 00:06:05,116
Heading, vector one,
nine degrees.

8
00:06:09,954 --> 00:06:12,582
We are prepped for lockdown.

9
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:19,422
I can't believe it,
this is ridiculous.

10
00:06:19,505 --> 00:06:21,609
I haven't got more than my hand in
the last six weeks,

11
00:06:21,633 --> 00:06:23,009
and now this shit.

12
00:06:24,594 --> 00:06:26,512
I mean, why can't we go to Mars,
captain?

13
00:06:26,596 --> 00:06:29,474
I mean, Mars has got women.

14
00:06:29,557 --> 00:06:31,017
Smith's right.

15
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:32,727
Neptune,
there's nothing out there.

16
00:06:32,852 --> 00:06:35,164
Smith: Yeah, and if the shit goes
down, we'll be on our own.

17
00:06:35,188 --> 00:06:36,940
You know the rules, people.

18
00:06:37,023 --> 00:06:39,025
Someone drops the ball,
we get the call.

19
00:06:39,108 --> 00:06:41,027
Now, let's go.

20
00:06:41,569 --> 00:06:43,329
Have you got our course plotted in?
Yes, sir.

21
00:06:43,446 --> 00:06:44,906
Locked and cooked
and ready to rock.

22
00:06:44,989 --> 00:06:45,990
Mr. Justin?

23
00:06:47,492 --> 00:06:49,092
Everything's green
on my screen, skipper.

24
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:50,387
Miller:
Start your countdown now.

25
00:06:50,411 --> 00:06:53,748
Ion drive will engage
in t minus 10 minutes.

26
00:06:53,831 --> 00:06:55,166
Thank you, lieutenant.

27
00:06:55,375 --> 00:06:57,168
Let's go. Smith, you follow me.

28
00:06:58,044 --> 00:06:59,462
Smith: Yes, ma'am.

29
00:06:59,754 --> 00:07:01,339
Miller: Justin,
get those tanks prepped.

30
00:07:01,422 --> 00:07:02,590
Justin: Yes, sir.

31
00:07:06,511 --> 00:07:08,179
Mr. Cooper. Cooper: Skipper.

32
00:07:08,262 --> 00:07:09,639
Ion drive in 10 minutes.

33
00:07:09,722 --> 00:07:11,641
Ooh, it's time to play
spam-in-the-can.

34
00:07:11,724 --> 00:07:14,936
Skipper. Miller: Peters.

35
00:07:15,019 --> 00:07:16,997
Captain Miller, I just wanted to say
this is something...

36
00:07:17,021 --> 00:07:18,398
Clock is ticking, doctor.

37
00:07:21,234 --> 00:07:23,337
You waiting for a personal
invitation, there, Mr. Smith?

38
00:07:23,361 --> 00:07:25,321
No, sir. Captain, I don't want to
take up any...

39
00:07:25,405 --> 00:07:28,199
Doctor, if you'll just follow the
rest of the crew to the grav tanks,

40
00:07:28,282 --> 00:07:29,492
we're almost underway.

41
00:07:29,575 --> 00:07:31,335
That bunk better be
squared away, Mr. Cooper,

42
00:07:31,536 --> 00:07:33,329
or you'll find yourself
walking to Neptune.

43
00:07:33,413 --> 00:07:34,664
What's the hold—up, Peters?

44
00:07:34,747 --> 00:07:37,709
Just loading the last of
the co2 scrubbers, sir.

45
00:07:37,792 --> 00:07:39,085
You stand right here.

46
00:07:42,547 --> 00:07:44,048
Smith: Thanks, d.J.

47
00:07:44,132 --> 00:07:45,550
Smith: Hats off in the tank.
Sir.

48
00:07:45,633 --> 00:07:47,385
First time in a grav couch?

49
00:07:47,468 --> 00:07:50,013
Yeah. Uh-huh.

50
00:07:50,096 --> 00:07:53,516
I think your captain has some kind of
a problem with me.

51
00:07:53,599 --> 00:07:55,601
Oh, well. Don't worry about him.

52
00:07:55,685 --> 00:07:58,312
He just loves having complete
strangers on his ship.

53
00:07:58,396 --> 00:08:00,148
D.j. D.J.: Yeah.

54
00:08:02,608 --> 00:08:03,735
No.

55
00:08:03,818 --> 00:08:05,653
Is this necessary?

56
00:08:05,737 --> 00:08:07,530
When the ion drive fires,

57
00:08:07,613 --> 00:08:09,449
you'll be taking about 30 g's.

58
00:08:10,408 --> 00:08:11,784
Without a tank,

59
00:08:11,868 --> 00:08:14,162
the force would
quuefy your skeleton.

60
00:08:15,705 --> 00:08:17,373
I've seen the effect on mice.

61
00:08:21,836 --> 00:08:23,212
Claustrophobic?

62
00:08:23,880 --> 00:08:25,381
Very. Hmm.

63
00:08:49,822 --> 00:08:51,157
Claire: Billy.

64
00:09:34,075 --> 00:09:36,035
I'm so alone.

65
00:09:39,831 --> 00:09:40,873
Hello?

66
00:09:50,216 --> 00:09:51,425
Is anyone there?

67
00:10:01,978 --> 00:10:03,312
Oh, god.

68
00:10:35,928 --> 00:10:37,221
Claire: Billy?

69
00:10:39,599 --> 00:10:40,975
I'm so cold.

70
00:10:51,027 --> 00:10:52,403
Claire.

71
00:10:55,198 --> 00:10:56,532
I'm waiting.

72
00:11:08,961 --> 00:11:11,088
You're all right. Breathe.

73
00:11:11,172 --> 00:11:13,633
D.j.i

74
00:11:13,716 --> 00:11:15,444
okay, Dr. Weir, let's get you up.
Okay, I'm fine.

75
00:11:15,468 --> 00:11:16,761
Come on, take it easy. I'm fine.

76
00:11:16,844 --> 00:11:18,846
Okay. There you go.

77
00:11:20,139 --> 00:11:21,349
Now move nice and slow.

78
00:11:21,432 --> 00:11:23,517
You've been in stasis
for 56 days.

79
00:11:23,601 --> 00:11:26,121
You're gonna experience a little
disorientation, you understand?

80
00:11:26,562 --> 00:11:27,897
I'm all right now, thank you.

81
00:11:27,980 --> 00:11:30,524
Okay, take that. Don't rush it.

82
00:11:30,608 --> 00:11:31,776
All yours.

83
00:11:36,072 --> 00:11:37,240
You okay, doc?

84
00:11:38,699 --> 00:11:39,825
Want some coffee?

85
00:11:40,576 --> 00:11:41,577
What?

86
00:11:42,328 --> 00:11:44,789
Would you like some coffee?

87
00:11:46,165 --> 00:11:48,918
No. Thank you.

88
00:11:49,001 --> 00:11:51,420
All right, suit yourself.

89
00:11:51,504 --> 00:11:53,130
What about you, starck?

90
00:11:53,214 --> 00:11:55,591
Would you like something
hot and black inside you?

91
00:11:55,675 --> 00:11:56,968
Ooh! Is that an offer?

92
00:11:57,051 --> 00:11:58,386
It is not.

93
00:11:58,469 --> 00:12:00,054
Then how about
some coffee, then?

94
00:12:00,137 --> 00:12:01,764
Starck? Oh.

95
00:12:01,847 --> 00:12:04,058
Yes, sir. Why aren't you on the
bndge?

96
00:12:04,141 --> 00:12:05,494
Do you mind
if I get dressed first?

97
00:12:05,518 --> 00:12:06,578
As a matter of fact, lieutenant,

98
00:12:06,602 --> 00:12:07,962
I do mind if you
get dressed first.

99
00:12:08,229 --> 00:12:11,065
Let's go, people.
We have a job to do.

100
00:12:11,148 --> 00:12:13,276
Peters: Okay, Denny, here we go.

101
00:12:13,359 --> 00:12:14,777
Happy birthday.

102
00:12:14,860 --> 00:12:16,779
Play horsey, mommy. Play horsey.

103
00:12:16,862 --> 00:12:19,115
Hey, no more ball in the house.

104
00:12:19,198 --> 00:12:20,533
Cooper: Sorry, mama.

105
00:12:20,616 --> 00:12:22,302
Justin: Yeah, you're the one who
can't catch.

106
00:12:22,326 --> 00:12:23,369
Cooper: Yo, apologize.

107
00:12:23,452 --> 00:12:25,621
Sorry about that, mama bean

108
00:12:26,622 --> 00:12:27,683
all right. All right, then.

109
00:12:27,707 --> 00:12:30,543
Miller: Uh, Peters. Yes, sir.

110
00:12:30,626 --> 00:12:32,795
I did try to find
a replacement for you,

111
00:12:32,878 --> 00:12:35,798
but with such short notice,
it was impossible.

112
00:12:35,881 --> 00:12:36,882
I am sorry.

113
00:12:37,466 --> 00:12:39,176
I talked to my ex.

114
00:12:39,260 --> 00:12:41,220
He's going to take
Denny for Christmas.

115
00:12:41,304 --> 00:12:43,097
I'll get him for the summer,

116
00:12:44,557 --> 00:12:45,725
so everything's all right.

117
00:12:46,517 --> 00:12:48,394
Cooper: Smitty.

118
00:12:48,477 --> 00:12:50,062
Excuse me, ladies and gentlemen,

119
00:12:50,146 --> 00:12:52,064
in approximately
two hours and 23 minutes,

120
00:12:52,148 --> 00:12:54,442
we'll be arriving in
the orbit of Neptune.

121
00:12:54,525 --> 00:12:55,818
Thank you very much.
I thank you.

122
00:12:55,901 --> 00:12:57,653
Ooh, that's good.

123
00:12:57,737 --> 00:13:00,281
All boards are green.
Everything's five by five.

124
00:13:00,364 --> 00:13:01,866
Usac been notified
of our position?

125
00:13:01,949 --> 00:13:03,117
Starck: Yes, sir.

126
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:04,910
All right, people, listen up. Smith:
D.J.

127
00:13:04,994 --> 00:13:07,139
Miller: As you all know, we have an
addition to our crew.

128
00:13:07,163 --> 00:13:08,914
Dr. Weir, you care to join us?

129
00:13:10,249 --> 00:13:11,667
Introductions all around.

130
00:13:11,751 --> 00:13:13,753
This is my xo,
lieutenant starck.

131
00:13:13,836 --> 00:13:15,546
Mr. Justin, engineering.

132
00:13:15,629 --> 00:13:17,340
The funky spaceman
over here is Mr. Cooper.

133
00:13:17,423 --> 00:13:19,550
What exactly is it you do
on board this ship, Coop?

134
00:13:19,633 --> 00:13:22,219
Listen up, doc,
I'm your best friend, okay?

135
00:13:22,303 --> 00:13:24,555
I am the lifesaver
and the heartbreaker.

136
00:13:24,638 --> 00:13:26,682
He's a rescue technician.

137
00:13:26,766 --> 00:13:28,559
This is Peters,
medical technician.

138
00:13:28,642 --> 00:13:32,146
And my pilot, Mr. Smith.

139
00:13:32,229 --> 00:13:34,440
The gloomy Gus in the corner over
there, that's...

140
00:13:34,523 --> 00:13:36,817
D.j. Trauma.

141
00:13:39,028 --> 00:13:40,654
Cooper: All right, all right.

142
00:13:40,738 --> 00:13:42,114
Now that we all know each other,

143
00:13:42,198 --> 00:13:43,800
skipper, I got a question.
Miller: Certainly.

144
00:13:43,824 --> 00:13:45,785
What the fuck are we doing
way out here?

145
00:13:45,868 --> 00:13:48,268
Well, perhaps the good doctor will be
kind enough to tell us.

146
00:13:49,705 --> 00:13:51,999
Thank you. Uh...

147
00:13:52,083 --> 00:13:53,810
First of all, I want to say how much
I appreciate

148
00:13:53,834 --> 00:13:55,503
this opportunity
to join you on what I...

149
00:13:55,586 --> 00:13:57,522
It's for sure that you appreciate
being here, doctor,

150
00:13:57,546 --> 00:13:59,840
but you must understand
that we don't.

151
00:13:59,924 --> 00:14:01,550
We were taken off
a well-deserved leave

152
00:14:01,634 --> 00:14:03,344
and sent into Neptune space.

153
00:14:03,427 --> 00:14:07,306
We are now three billion klicks from
the nearest outpost.

154
00:14:07,390 --> 00:14:09,141
The last time usac
attempted a rescue

155
00:14:09,225 --> 00:14:10,559
this far past the outer reach,

156
00:14:10,643 --> 00:14:13,729
well, we lost both ships.
80, if you please...

157
00:14:14,522 --> 00:14:17,191
Right. Well...

158
00:14:17,274 --> 00:14:21,112
Everything I'm about to tell you is
considered code black by the NSA.

159
00:14:21,195 --> 00:14:23,656
Usac intercepted
a radio transmission

160
00:14:23,739 --> 00:14:27,159
from a decaying orbit
around Neptune.

161
00:14:27,243 --> 00:14:30,871
The source of this transmission has
beenidenfified

162
00:14:30,955 --> 00:14:32,331
as the event horizon.

163
00:14:35,042 --> 00:14:37,336
Yeah, well, that's bullshit, for
starters.

164
00:14:37,420 --> 00:14:38,700
Skipper,
you took me off leave...

165
00:14:38,754 --> 00:14:40,256
Thank you so much.

166
00:14:40,339 --> 00:14:41,757
Smitty, sit down.

167
00:14:43,926 --> 00:14:46,011
Cooper, as you were.

168
00:14:47,221 --> 00:14:48,639
Let the man speak.

169
00:14:48,722 --> 00:14:50,724
Weir: What was made public about the
event horizon,

170
00:14:50,808 --> 00:14:53,185
that she was a deep space
research vessel,

171
00:14:53,269 --> 00:14:55,729
that her reactor went critical,

172
00:14:55,813 --> 00:14:57,314
and that the ship blew up,

173
00:14:57,398 --> 00:14:58,816
none of that is true.

174
00:14:58,899 --> 00:15:00,901
The event horizon
was the culmination

175
00:15:00,985 --> 00:15:03,612
of a secret government project to
create a spacecraft

176
00:15:03,696 --> 00:15:05,906
capable of
faster-than-light flight.

177
00:15:05,990 --> 00:15:07,575
Smith: I don't... excuse me.

178
00:15:07,658 --> 00:15:09,618
See, you can't actually do that.

179
00:15:09,702 --> 00:15:13,706
The law of relativity prohibits
faster-than-light travel.

180
00:15:13,789 --> 00:15:18,169
Relativity, yes. We can't break the
law of relativity.

181
00:15:18,252 --> 00:15:20,129
We can go around it.

182
00:15:20,212 --> 00:15:21,731
The ship doesn't really
go faster than light.

183
00:15:21,755 --> 00:15:24,925
What it does is it creates
a dimensional gateway,

184
00:15:25,009 --> 00:15:27,094
that allows it to
jump instantaneously

185
00:15:27,178 --> 00:15:29,513
from one point of the universe to
another light years away.

186
00:15:29,597 --> 00:15:31,265
How?

187
00:15:31,348 --> 00:15:33,934
Well, it's... it's difficult to...
It's all math.

188
00:15:34,018 --> 00:15:35,478
Try us, doctor.

189
00:15:36,353 --> 00:15:39,190
Right, well, uh,
using layman's terms,

190
00:15:39,482 --> 00:15:41,692
we use a rotating magnetic field

191
00:15:41,775 --> 00:15:43,444
to focus a narrow beam
of gravitons.

192
00:15:43,527 --> 00:15:46,030
These in turn fold space-time

193
00:15:46,113 --> 00:15:48,282
consistent with
weyl tensor dynamics,

194
00:15:48,365 --> 00:15:51,118
until the space—time curvature
becomes infinitely large

195
00:15:51,202 --> 00:15:53,787
and you produce a singularity. Now,
the singularity...

196
00:15:53,871 --> 00:15:55,247
Layman's terms.

197
00:15:55,331 --> 00:15:59,168
Well, fuck layman's terms.
Do you speak English?

198
00:15:59,251 --> 00:16:00,377
Um...

199
00:16:02,838 --> 00:16:05,966
Imagine, for a minute,
that this piece of paper...

200
00:16:06,050 --> 00:16:09,303
Excuse me. Uh, that's Vanessa, and
that's mine. Uh...

201
00:16:09,386 --> 00:16:12,223
Attractive piece of paper
represents space-time,

202
00:16:12,306 --> 00:16:14,934
and you want to get
from point "a" here...

203
00:16:15,017 --> 00:16:16,310
Oh...

204
00:16:16,393 --> 00:16:18,437
To point "b" there.

205
00:16:18,521 --> 00:16:21,315
Now, what's the shortest distance
between two points?

206
00:16:21,398 --> 00:16:22,399
A straight line.

207
00:16:24,026 --> 00:16:25,319
Wrong.

208
00:16:25,402 --> 00:16:27,738
The shortest distance
between two points is zero.

209
00:16:27,821 --> 00:16:29,698
And that's what
the gateway does.

210
00:16:30,324 --> 00:16:33,619
It folds space,

211
00:16:33,702 --> 00:16:38,332
so that point "a" and point "b"
co-exist in the same space and time.

212
00:16:39,291 --> 00:16:43,379
When the spacecraft
passes through the gateway,

213
00:16:43,462 --> 00:16:45,881
space returns to normal.

214
00:16:45,965 --> 00:16:48,217
It's called a gravity drive.

215
00:16:49,301 --> 00:16:50,886
Justin:
How do you know all this?

216
00:16:51,637 --> 00:16:53,347
I built it.

217
00:16:53,639 --> 00:16:55,599
Well, I can see
why they sent you.

218
00:16:55,683 --> 00:16:58,477
So if the ship didn't blow up, then
what happened?

219
00:16:58,561 --> 00:17:02,565
Well, the mission was going
perfectly, like a textbook.

220
00:17:02,648 --> 00:17:05,192
They reached safe distance
using conventional thrusters.

221
00:17:05,276 --> 00:17:07,152
All systems looked good.

222
00:17:07,236 --> 00:17:09,196
They had the go—ahead
to use the gravity drive

223
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:11,198
to open the gateway
to proxima centauri,

224
00:17:12,575 --> 00:17:15,244
and then, they just disappeared.

225
00:17:15,327 --> 00:17:19,373
Vanished without a trace,
until now.

226
00:17:19,456 --> 00:17:21,792
Where's she been for the last seven
years, doctor?

227
00:17:22,835 --> 00:17:25,546
That's what we're here
to find out.

228
00:17:25,629 --> 00:17:29,675
Now, we've been unable
to verify live contact,

229
00:17:29,758 --> 00:17:33,095
but tdrs did receive
this single transmission.

230
00:17:44,273 --> 00:17:45,482
What the fucking hell is that?

231
00:17:45,566 --> 00:17:46,942
Listen to this.

232
00:17:47,026 --> 00:17:50,154
Houston passed the same recording
through several filters,

233
00:17:50,237 --> 00:17:54,116
managed to isolate what appears to be
a human voice.

234
00:18:02,583 --> 00:18:04,627
I'm not even sure
if it qualifies as language.

235
00:18:04,710 --> 00:18:06,378
Latin. What?

236
00:18:07,546 --> 00:18:10,049
I mean, I think
it sounds like Latin.

237
00:18:10,132 --> 00:18:11,300
Can you translate it?

238
00:18:11,383 --> 00:18:12,718
Play it again.

239
00:18:17,139 --> 00:18:18,849
Right there. You hear that?

240
00:18:22,561 --> 00:18:26,148
Sounds like, "liberate me,"

241
00:18:26,440 --> 00:18:28,126
something. I don't know.
I can't make out the rest.

242
00:18:28,150 --> 00:18:29,485
"Liberate me?"

243
00:18:31,070 --> 00:18:32,655
"Save me."

244
00:18:34,657 --> 00:18:36,158
Miller: All right.
Stations, people.

245
00:18:50,464 --> 00:18:52,132
Crossing the horizon.

246
00:18:52,216 --> 00:18:54,718
Optimum approach angle
is 14 degrees.

247
00:18:54,802 --> 00:18:56,804
Miller: Come around to 334.

248
00:18:56,887 --> 00:18:58,222
Heading 334, sir.

249
00:18:58,305 --> 00:19:01,100
Make your approach vector negative,
14 degrees.

250
00:19:01,392 --> 00:19:02,476
1-4 degrees.

251
00:19:06,355 --> 00:19:10,275
We have a lock on event horizon's
navigation beacon.

252
00:19:10,359 --> 00:19:12,444
She's in the upper ionosphere and it
looks like...

253
00:19:12,528 --> 00:19:14,780
We're in for some very
heavy chop, sir.

254
00:19:18,992 --> 00:19:21,662
Bring us in nice and tight, Mr.
Smith. Sir.

255
00:19:21,745 --> 00:19:23,455
Mr. Justin, how's my ship?

256
00:19:23,539 --> 00:19:24,998
Smooth sailing, sir.

257
00:19:26,083 --> 00:19:27,918
Matching speed on my Mark.

258
00:19:28,001 --> 00:19:31,797
Three, two, one. Mark.

259
00:19:36,051 --> 00:19:37,052
Range?

260
00:19:37,136 --> 00:19:39,513
10,000 meters and closing, sir.

261
00:19:39,596 --> 00:19:40,824
Miller: Starck.
Starck: Yes, sir.

262
00:19:40,848 --> 00:19:42,683
Get on the horn.
See if anybody is listening.

263
00:19:42,766 --> 00:19:45,644
This is the us. Aerospace command
vessel Lewis & Clark

264
00:19:45,728 --> 00:19:47,104
hailing event horizon.

265
00:19:47,187 --> 00:19:48,856
Event horizon, do you read?

266
00:19:48,939 --> 00:19:50,149
Miller: Dr. Weir.

267
00:19:51,483 --> 00:19:53,569
I think you'll want to see this.

268
00:19:53,652 --> 00:19:54,862
Where is she?

269
00:19:54,945 --> 00:19:56,822
Dead ahead. 5,000 meters!

270
00:19:56,905 --> 00:19:58,157
Starck? Anybody home?

271
00:19:58,240 --> 00:19:59,867
Event horizon, do you read?

272
00:19:59,950 --> 00:20:01,410
3,000 meters and closing!

273
00:20:06,540 --> 00:20:07,708
I can't see a thing.

274
00:20:07,791 --> 00:20:10,252
1,500 meters, sir.
We're getting too close.

275
00:20:10,335 --> 00:20:11,503
Where is it?

276
00:20:11,587 --> 00:20:13,630
The scope is lit.
It's right in front of us.

277
00:20:13,714 --> 00:20:15,090
1,000 meters.

278
00:20:15,174 --> 00:20:16,550
Proximity warning!

279
00:20:16,633 --> 00:20:20,220
900, 800, 700.

280
00:20:20,304 --> 00:20:22,765
We're right on top of her, sir!
Miller: We're gonna hit.

281
00:20:22,848 --> 00:20:24,892
Starck! It's right there! God!

282
00:20:27,561 --> 00:20:29,313
Reverse thrusters. Full!

283
00:20:42,910 --> 00:20:44,077
There she is.

284
00:21:04,723 --> 00:21:06,225
Justin: Jesus.

285
00:21:06,308 --> 00:21:08,811
Very impressive ship, doctor.

286
00:21:09,520 --> 00:21:10,646
Thank you.

287
00:21:10,729 --> 00:21:13,941
Mr. Smith,
are you up for a flyby?

288
00:21:14,024 --> 00:21:15,692
Yes, sir. Yes, I'd love to.

289
00:21:30,499 --> 00:21:33,043
That's the main airlock,
we can dock in there.

290
00:21:33,126 --> 00:21:34,962
All right, Smith.

291
00:21:35,045 --> 00:21:38,382
Use the arm and lock us onto that
small antenna cluster.

292
00:21:46,098 --> 00:21:47,283
We should go
very carefully here.

293
00:21:47,307 --> 00:21:48,851
That is not
a load-bearing structure.

294
00:21:51,520 --> 00:21:52,813
Is now, doctor.

295
00:21:56,441 --> 00:21:58,026
Everything five by five?

296
00:21:58,110 --> 00:21:59,695
Locked in to the event horizon.

297
00:21:59,778 --> 00:22:01,905
Thank you, smitty.
Light 'em if you got 'em.

298
00:22:01,989 --> 00:22:03,282
Thank you very much, sir.

299
00:22:05,409 --> 00:22:07,119
Starck, give me a read.

300
00:22:07,202 --> 00:22:08,787
The reactor's still hot.

301
00:22:08,871 --> 00:22:11,373
Uh, we've got several small radiation
sources but no leaks.

302
00:22:11,456 --> 00:22:12,934
Probably it's nothing serious.

303
00:22:12,958 --> 00:22:14,477
Miller: Do they have pressure?
Affirmative.

304
00:22:14,501 --> 00:22:17,045
The hull's intact, uh,
but there's no gravity,

305
00:22:17,129 --> 00:22:19,423
and the thermal units
are off-line.

306
00:22:19,506 --> 00:22:21,216
I'm showing deep cold.

307
00:22:21,300 --> 00:22:23,510
The crew couldn't survive
unless they were in stasis.

308
00:22:23,594 --> 00:22:25,137
Find 'em, starck.

309
00:22:25,220 --> 00:22:27,501
Yeah, I'm already on it, captain.
Bio-scan is on the line.

310
00:22:30,934 --> 00:22:32,414
There's something
wrong with the scan.

311
00:22:32,477 --> 00:22:33,979
Is it radiation interference?

312
00:22:34,062 --> 00:22:35,623
Starck: There's not enough
to throw it off.

313
00:22:35,647 --> 00:22:37,399
I'm picking up trace life forms,

314
00:22:37,482 --> 00:22:39,735
but I can't get
a lock on their location.

315
00:22:39,818 --> 00:22:42,213
These readings are all over the ship.
It doesn't make any sense.

316
00:22:42,237 --> 00:22:45,782
Okay, we do it the hard way.
Deck by deck, room by room.

317
00:22:45,866 --> 00:22:47,701
Starck, deploy the umbilicus.

318
00:22:47,784 --> 00:22:49,661
Smitty, fire up the boards
in the war room.

319
00:22:49,745 --> 00:22:51,163
Sir.

320
00:22:51,246 --> 00:22:53,707
Mr. Justin, I believe
you're up for a walk?

321
00:22:53,790 --> 00:22:54,958
Yes, sir.

322
00:22:59,254 --> 00:23:01,089
Doctor, I'm gonna need you
here on the bridge.

323
00:23:01,173 --> 00:23:05,218
Captain, I didn't come out all this
way just to sit on your bridge.

324
00:23:05,302 --> 00:23:06,595
I need to be on that ship.

325
00:23:06,678 --> 00:23:08,615
Once we've secured the ship,
we'll bring you on board.

326
00:23:08,639 --> 00:23:10,450
No, I'm sorry, that's unacceptable. I
need to be on that ship.

327
00:23:10,474 --> 00:23:13,435
Once we've secured the ship.
That's the way it is.

328
00:23:13,518 --> 00:23:15,204
I need you to guide us
from the com station.

329
00:23:15,228 --> 00:23:16,605
This is where I need you.

330
00:23:16,688 --> 00:23:17,981
Help us do ourjob.

331
00:23:31,328 --> 00:23:34,414
Oh, oh, oh! Honey, honey,
you forgot your briefcase.

332
00:23:46,134 --> 00:23:47,302
Stand clear.

333
00:23:49,680 --> 00:23:51,682
Anything gets funky, Cooper...

334
00:23:51,765 --> 00:23:52,933
I'll be on it.

335
00:23:54,935 --> 00:23:57,813
Yo, baby bear,
keep your nose clean.

336
00:24:05,529 --> 00:24:07,114
D.j., showtime.

337
00:24:09,491 --> 00:24:11,243
Video feed is clear.

338
00:24:11,326 --> 00:24:13,203
Are you with us, Dr. Weir?

339
00:24:13,286 --> 00:24:15,789
Yes, I'm... I'm with you.

340
00:24:15,872 --> 00:24:17,624
Miller: We are crossing the
umbilicus,

341
00:24:17,708 --> 00:24:19,668
making our approach
to the event horizon.

342
00:24:22,838 --> 00:24:24,297
You're at the outer
airlock door.

343
00:24:26,925 --> 00:24:28,802
Miller: Magnetic boots on.

344
00:24:34,349 --> 00:24:35,684
Peters: We've got pressure.

345
00:24:55,620 --> 00:24:57,330
Miller: Place is a deep freeze.

346
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,002
We got ice crystals everywhere.

347
00:25:03,712 --> 00:25:06,006
That's the central corridor
that you're in now.

348
00:25:06,089 --> 00:25:08,675
It connects the personnel areas at
the front of the ship

349
00:25:08,759 --> 00:25:10,177
to engineering at the rear.

350
00:25:12,637 --> 00:25:14,237
Miller: Mr. Justin,
you take engineering.

351
00:25:14,264 --> 00:25:15,474
Justin: Yes, sir.

352
00:25:15,557 --> 00:25:17,601
Miller: Peters and I'll take
the forward decks.

353
00:25:28,320 --> 00:25:29,863
Miller: Doctor, what are these?

354
00:25:31,239 --> 00:25:33,158
Peters: Yeah, I've got
another one over here.

355
00:25:33,241 --> 00:25:34,701
They are all over the place.

356
00:25:34,785 --> 00:25:36,369
In an emergency,

357
00:25:36,453 --> 00:25:40,582
they destroy the central corridor and
split the ship in half,

358
00:25:40,665 --> 00:25:43,668
and then the crew can use
the foredecks as a lifeboat.

359
00:25:49,257 --> 00:25:52,761
Miller: I'm in medical.
No casualties.

360
00:25:56,932 --> 00:25:59,142
Place looks like
it's never been used.

361
00:25:59,226 --> 00:26:00,661
Weir: You still haven't
seen any crew?

362
00:26:00,685 --> 00:26:03,146
If we saw any crew, doctor,
you'd know it by now.

363
00:26:04,981 --> 00:26:06,650
Scanning for life readings.

364
00:26:10,612 --> 00:26:12,447
This place is a tomb.

365
00:26:15,450 --> 00:26:16,785
Oh! Fuck me!

366
00:26:18,036 --> 00:26:19,830
Starck:
Miller. Miller, are you okay?

367
00:26:19,913 --> 00:26:22,958
Looks like skipper
got a case of the willies.

368
00:26:23,041 --> 00:26:25,001
Starck: Cooper,
get back to your post.

369
00:26:25,085 --> 00:26:27,462
Miller, your heart rate
just went through the roof.

370
00:26:27,546 --> 00:26:28,755
I'm fine.

371
00:26:35,387 --> 00:26:36,572
Justin: I think
I've reached the door

372
00:26:36,596 --> 00:26:39,349
to the first containment,
Dr. Weir.

373
00:26:39,432 --> 00:26:41,476
Weir: The engineering decks are on
the other side.

374
00:26:41,560 --> 00:26:43,061
Justin: Kay, let's take a look.

375
00:27:00,996 --> 00:27:04,541
What the hell
is this place, Dr. Weir?

376
00:27:04,624 --> 00:27:06,384
It allows you to enter
the second containment

377
00:27:06,418 --> 00:27:08,503
without compromising
the magnetic fields.

378
00:27:10,755 --> 00:27:12,883
Looks like a meat grinder to me.

379
00:27:17,345 --> 00:27:18,680
Peters: I'm on the bridge.

380
00:27:26,855 --> 00:27:28,023
Got some blood here.

381
00:27:42,787 --> 00:27:44,664
Justin: There must have been
a coolant leak.

382
00:27:44,748 --> 00:27:46,499
Man, this shit is everywhere.

383
00:27:48,960 --> 00:27:52,464
Coolant level's on reserve, but well
within the safety line.

384
00:28:04,559 --> 00:28:06,978
Miss Peters,
could you turn back and left?

385
00:28:08,813 --> 00:28:10,315
Starck: What is it?

386
00:28:10,398 --> 00:28:11,650
Ship's log.

387
00:28:31,419 --> 00:28:32,587
Peters: It's stuck.

388
00:28:34,923 --> 00:28:37,175
It's pretty jammed in there.

389
00:28:42,472 --> 00:28:43,682
Starck: Oh, my god.

390
00:28:46,059 --> 00:28:48,436
What the hell
happened to his eyes?

391
00:28:48,520 --> 00:28:50,438
Explosive decompression.

392
00:28:50,522 --> 00:28:52,399
Decompression doesn't do that.

393
00:28:54,025 --> 00:28:55,986
D.j.: Looks like it was caused by...

394
00:28:56,069 --> 00:28:57,696
I don't know,
an animal or something.

395
00:28:57,779 --> 00:29:00,991
Look at the damage to the soft
tissue, massive abrasions.

396
00:29:02,158 --> 00:29:03,410
Corpse-sicle.

397
00:29:06,162 --> 00:29:07,956
Justin: I'm scanning for
life forms.

398
00:29:13,628 --> 00:29:17,132
Man, I'm getting some really strange
readings in here.

399
00:29:29,227 --> 00:29:31,062
Let me see if I can
get some power up.

400
00:29:42,907 --> 00:29:44,826
That's the core,
the gravity drive.

401
00:29:51,082 --> 00:29:52,375
The heart of the ship.

402
00:30:08,641 --> 00:30:10,185
Jusfin.

403
00:30:10,268 --> 00:30:11,853
Justin, you're breaking up.

404
00:30:22,906 --> 00:30:24,157
Justin, come in.

405
00:30:36,127 --> 00:30:37,420
What is it? I don't know.

406
00:30:37,504 --> 00:30:39,297
The life scan just
went off the scale.

407
00:30:50,600 --> 00:30:52,720
Something's wrong, starck.
Pull him out. Pull him out.

408
00:30:58,983 --> 00:31:00,235
Oh, shit!

409
00:31:04,948 --> 00:31:06,074
He's in trouble.

410
00:31:06,157 --> 00:31:07,492
Go, Coop. I'm gone.

411
00:31:46,906 --> 00:31:49,200
Miller:
Boarding party, sound off.

412
00:31:49,284 --> 00:31:51,995
This is Miller.
Can anybody hear me?

413
00:31:58,585 --> 00:32:00,211
What the fuck was that?

414
00:32:00,295 --> 00:32:03,256
Pressure emergency! We've lost the
starboard baffle.

415
00:32:03,339 --> 00:32:04,757
The hull's been breached!

416
00:32:06,176 --> 00:32:08,553
I'll contain it here for as long as I
can! Get out!

417
00:32:08,636 --> 00:32:10,555
We're losing atmosphere.

418
00:32:12,557 --> 00:32:14,976
There are pressure suits
in the airlock. Go!

419
00:32:15,059 --> 00:32:16,936
Cooper:
Here I come. Here I come.

420
00:32:21,441 --> 00:32:23,485
Hold on, baby bear.

421
00:32:23,568 --> 00:32:25,445
I'm almost there, almost there.

422
00:32:30,366 --> 00:32:31,576
I got you. I got...

423
00:32:33,244 --> 00:32:34,954
Watch out. Watch...

424
00:32:36,414 --> 00:32:39,375
I got you.
What the fuck is this?

425
00:32:39,459 --> 00:32:40,502
Hold on.

426
00:32:43,880 --> 00:32:47,217
Captain Miller. Captain Miller, do
you copy? Over.

427
00:32:47,300 --> 00:32:48,927
Smitty,
where you been all my life?

428
00:32:49,010 --> 00:32:50,970
We have a major
situation here, sir.

429
00:32:51,054 --> 00:32:53,890
We've lost the starboard baffle, and
the hull's been breached.

430
00:32:53,973 --> 00:32:56,809
Uh, the safety circuits,
they have failed.

431
00:32:56,893 --> 00:32:59,145
Do we have time for a weld?
No, sir.

432
00:32:59,229 --> 00:33:03,233
We've got 218 liters of gas left,
sir, and then we've got nothing.

433
00:33:03,316 --> 00:33:05,902
The oxygen tanks, they failed,
they're broken.

434
00:33:05,985 --> 00:33:07,737
Sir, we are fucking dead!

435
00:33:07,820 --> 00:33:09,239
What about the event horizon?

436
00:33:09,739 --> 00:33:11,074
What?

437
00:33:11,157 --> 00:33:12,867
She's got air and power.

438
00:33:12,951 --> 00:33:14,077
It's our only chance.

439
00:33:14,160 --> 00:33:16,204
There's no way
I'm getting on that bastard.

440
00:33:16,287 --> 00:33:17,932
We don't even know
what happened on that ship.

441
00:33:17,956 --> 00:33:20,208
It beats dying, Mr. Smith.

442
00:33:20,291 --> 00:33:24,212
I want all personnel on board
the event horizon, pronto.

443
00:33:24,295 --> 00:33:25,922
We'll meet at the air tanks.

444
00:33:26,005 --> 00:33:30,051
I'm ahead of you, sir. Bringing all
thermal units online.

445
00:33:30,134 --> 00:33:33,388
Hold tight. Prep for g's.

446
00:33:33,471 --> 00:33:35,348
Gravity in five seconds.

447
00:33:50,405 --> 00:33:52,282
Cooper: Wake up, open your eyes. All
right?

448
00:33:52,365 --> 00:33:53,908
Just hang in there.

449
00:34:17,974 --> 00:34:19,100
Dr. Weir.

450
00:34:20,768 --> 00:34:22,687
Well...

451
00:34:22,770 --> 00:34:25,773
I brought most
primary systems online

452
00:34:25,857 --> 00:34:27,108
for now. Thank you.

453
00:34:27,191 --> 00:34:28,818
Starck, what's our status?

454
00:34:28,901 --> 00:34:30,987
The antenna array
is completely fried.

455
00:34:31,070 --> 00:34:34,282
We've got no radio,
no laser, no high gain.

456
00:34:34,365 --> 00:34:36,534
No one's coming to help us.

457
00:34:36,618 --> 00:34:39,120
This air tastes bad.
Yeah, carbon dioxide, I know.

458
00:34:39,203 --> 00:34:41,581
It's building up
with every breath we take.

459
00:34:41,664 --> 00:34:44,292
And the co2 filters
on the event horizon are shot.

460
00:34:44,375 --> 00:34:46,729
So, we take the scrubbers from the
Clark. That gives us what?

461
00:34:46,753 --> 00:34:48,838
Enough breathable
air for 20 hours,

462
00:34:48,921 --> 00:34:51,090
but after that, we'd better be on our
way home.

463
00:35:07,607 --> 00:35:09,043
Smith: I'm on the port side of the
hull,

464
00:35:09,067 --> 00:35:10,735
approaching
the dorsal heat sink.

465
00:35:15,281 --> 00:35:16,616
Holy shit.

466
00:35:22,705 --> 00:35:25,249
Captain Miller. Captain Miller, do
you copy? Over.

467
00:35:25,333 --> 00:35:27,794
I copy, Mr. Smith.
How's my ship?

468
00:35:27,877 --> 00:35:30,797
Sir, we've got a seven—meter fracture
on the outer hull.

469
00:35:30,880 --> 00:35:32,173
We should be able to repair her,

470
00:35:32,256 --> 00:35:33,817
but it's gonna take
an awful lot of time, sir.

471
00:35:33,841 --> 00:35:36,386
Negative.
20 hours, we run out of air.

472
00:35:36,469 --> 00:35:37,929
Understood, sir.

473
00:35:40,848 --> 00:35:42,850
What happened here, doctor?

474
00:35:54,028 --> 00:35:56,948
Okay, how is he?

475
00:35:57,031 --> 00:36:00,910
His vital signs seem stable, but he's
unresponsive to stimuli.

476
00:36:01,994 --> 00:36:03,162
I don't know.

477
00:36:07,542 --> 00:36:09,335
He might wake up in 15 minutes.

478
00:36:11,629 --> 00:36:12,797
He might not wake up at all.

479
00:36:14,132 --> 00:36:15,892
Look, skipper, I know
this shit sounds weird,

480
00:36:15,967 --> 00:36:17,969
but, I mean,
there was like nothing,

481
00:36:18,052 --> 00:36:21,764
and then Justin appeared
and_.And...

482
00:36:21,848 --> 00:36:25,226
It was liquid, and the whole core
just turned solid.

483
00:36:25,309 --> 00:36:27,270
It's just not
physically possible.

484
00:36:27,353 --> 00:36:30,064
Doctor, please. Please, don't start
in with that physics shit.

485
00:36:30,148 --> 00:36:32,066
I'm telling you, I saw it.

486
00:36:32,150 --> 00:36:34,861
Mr. Cooper, it would mean
the gateway was open.

487
00:36:34,944 --> 00:36:36,988
That's it. The gateway was open.

488
00:36:37,071 --> 00:36:38,656
The gateway couldn't be open

489
00:36:38,740 --> 00:36:40,950
because the gravity drive
wasn't activated.

490
00:36:41,033 --> 00:36:43,077
Look, I'm telling you
what I saw, doc! 80...

491
00:36:43,161 --> 00:36:44,847
It can't just start
up by itself.

492
00:36:44,871 --> 00:36:48,583
Mr. Cooper, you are out of line.

493
00:36:48,666 --> 00:36:51,794
Doctor, Mr. Justin may die.
Now, I have to assume

494
00:36:51,878 --> 00:36:54,213
that whatever happened to him
could happen to all of us.

495
00:36:55,381 --> 00:36:57,717
Mr. Cooper says
he saw something.

496
00:36:57,800 --> 00:36:59,677
I believe he saw something.

497
00:37:01,095 --> 00:37:03,014
I need an explanation.

498
00:37:06,768 --> 00:37:09,103
In my view,
Mr. Cooper's delusional,

499
00:37:09,187 --> 00:37:11,022
if you don't mind my saying so.

500
00:37:11,105 --> 00:37:13,107
But maybe he saw

501
00:37:13,191 --> 00:37:16,444
an optical effect caused
by gravitational distortion.

502
00:37:16,527 --> 00:37:18,321
"An optical effect"?
That's fucking poetic.

503
00:37:18,404 --> 00:37:20,031
Where the fuck... Cooper!

504
00:37:20,740 --> 00:37:22,325
I'm sorry. All right.

505
00:37:24,786 --> 00:37:26,245
Gravitational distortion.

506
00:37:26,329 --> 00:37:27,914
What could create that, doctor?

507
00:37:27,997 --> 00:37:32,460
If, somehow, a burst of gravity waves
escaped from the core,

508
00:37:33,503 --> 00:37:35,213
it could distort space-time.

509
00:37:35,296 --> 00:37:37,256
It could make Justin
seem to disappear.

510
00:37:37,340 --> 00:37:40,384
It could also have damaged
the Lewis & Clark.

511
00:37:41,928 --> 00:37:44,055
However, I think
this is entirely unlikely.

512
